use "testing" ;
use "radix-tree" ;

\ `when` and `unless` hand the caller's quotation to `if` as a value. Compiled on their own, their
\ arm's stack effect is whatever that quotation does, which the empty literal arm beside it says
\ nothing about.
\
\ `while` runs its predicate once before it calls `when`, so the loop quotation `when` receives
\ consumes the predicate's extra output. Warming `count-down` compiles `when` standalone.

step?:      ( n -- ..a ? )   [ dup 0 > [ dup 10 * t ] [ f ] if ] ;
advance:    ( n extra -- n ) [ drop 1 - ] ;
count-down: ( n -- n )       [ [ step? ] [ advance ] while ] ;

200 [ 3 count-down drop ] times

push-when: no-compile ( ? q -- ..a ) [ when ] ;
push-unless: no-compile ( ? q -- ..a ) [ unless ] ;

200 [ t [ ] push-when f [ ] push-unless ] times

"a loop quotation that consumes the predicate's extra output" test-section
100 5 count-down
0 "the loop ends at zero" assert=
100 "the value below the loop is untouched" assert=

"an arm that leaves more than the empty arm" test-section
100 t [ 5 6 ] push-when
6 "the top pushed value" assert=
5 "the next pushed value" assert=
100 "the value below is untouched" assert=
100 f [ 7 ] push-unless
7 "unless leaves its pushed value" assert=
100 "the value below unless is untouched" assert=

"an arm that consumes below it" test-section
100 1 2 t [ + ] push-when
3 "when consumed two and pushed their sum" assert=
100 "the value below the sum is untouched" assert=

"an untaken arm's quotation is released" test-section
100 [ f 7 [ drop 5 ] curry push-when ] times
100 f 7 [ drop 5 ] curry push-when
100 "a false condition leaves the stack as it was" assert=

"a radix lookup after its walk loop compiled" test-section
200 [
  string <radix-tree> "roman" 1 radix-insert "roast" 2 radix-insert
  dup "roman" radix-lookup? drop "roast" radix-lookup? drop
] times
string <radix-tree> "roman" 1 radix-insert "roast" 2 radix-insert
dup "roman" radix-lookup? option:some> 1 "the split key reads back" assert=
"roast" radix-lookup? option:some> 2 "the forked key reads back" assert=
